{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner completed a fresh bounded acceptance trace: frozen plan 005's initial architecture and Story 2/implementation handoff; R2 at 03cb4ee5 complete section 6, complete section 7.4 and complete sections 10.5–10.6; R3 at 80f390fc complete section 4.3 and complete sections 6.4–6.8. No whole-current-note rereading, ordinary verdict or implementation proof is claimed. This trace carries the user-facing waiting/pause/cancellation requirements into the existing IA/I3/I5 and joint acceptance work, without another task, suite, provider probe or design filing.\n\nThe browser must keep four separate records visible when relevant: task control/runtime application; the accepted commitment and its current hold; the merge item and destination publication update; the withdrawal send and its own recorded result. Pause stops new tools, saves work and lets the hold expire while responsibility remains. Task cancellation reconciles pending effects and does not itself withdraw a commitment or a merge. Closing the browser changes none of these. An accepted withdrawal result alone does not free a lane: the final publication update or reserve result must make the merge item final. Conversely, a final merge item frees the lane even while the cancellation result is still owed. A late result is matched to its original request/item, not a later merge.\n\nThe existing acceptance cases distinguish: (1) pause followed by hold expiry and resume into a new hold with retained work; (2) task cancellation while a commitment remains accepted; (3) withdrawal accepted with the result arriving before its final update; (4) the final update arriving first while its result remains pending; (5) withdrawal refused as reserved, versus refused as ended/not-reserved/out-of-date; (6) an unknown publication after a forwarded update or failed read, which cannot be labelled cancelled or released merely by stopping compute or revoking a credential. These are a mapping to existing plan/R0/R2/R3/R4 outcomes, not an added run quota. Reuse the existing both-order tables and real publication boundary when commissioning joint acceptance.\n\nR3's read-back rule records published only when the branch holds the integration commit. That final effect does not identify or settle an earlier unknown attempt, its token, or cleanup duties; those records stay owed. A compromised-key fence likewise requires real host compare-and-swap and exclusive-branch assumptions H1/H4, and is not a UI promise or proof supplied by finite race fixtures. Ordinary retired-key revocation after reservation does not retract the destination's already admitted authority. Full operation/attempt settlement form reconciliation remains G1/G2/G3; the multi-value ended reason remains G8. Their owners are unchanged, and no candidate-only addition is treated as adopted.\n\nSource/runtime acceptance and the real browser journey remain open. The queued C2 original full scope, highest-priority useful-test economy, builder-judged Jam entry and complete retarget/removal programme are preserved. No tests, builds, source edits, project scripts, provider/private diagnostics or external effects were executed.","body":{"no_git_artifact":"true","scope":"Bounded cancellation and publication acceptance trace for the existing connected browser stories"}}